[32m+[m[32m## Rate-limit demonstration[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe demo uses a sliding window of 60 seconds:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m| Endpoint group | Key | Limit |[m
[32m+[m[32m|---|---|---:|[m
[32m+[m[32m| `POST /api/session` | Direct client IP | 5 requests/minute |[m
[32m+[m[32m| `GET /api/records/:recordId` | Direct client IP + stable authenticated session ID | 10 requests/minute |[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mEach counted request receives `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `X-RateLimit-Reset` headers. A blocked request gets HTTP `429 Too Many Requests` and `Retry-After`. Login attempts are counted before password hashing so a burst is throttled before more expensive credential verification. The server does not trust `X-Forwarded-For`; it binds to `127.0.0.1` and uses the direct connection address. In production behind a trusted proxy, configure trusted proxy hops explicitly instead of trusting arbitrary forwarded headers.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mYou can see the 60-second behavior with cURL. If the rate-limit window is fresh, run six quick login attempts; the first five should be `200` and the sixth should be `429`. Any previous login attempts from the same IP also count toward the five-request limit:[m
